# SignalRound's settings. Copy this file to .env (`cp .env.example .env`). The native collector and local app
# need no API key: `pnpm collect:native -- --apply --no-jina` uses public feeds and direct HTTP only.
# A production deployment should set CRON_SECRET, DASHBOARD_PASSWORD, and SIGNAL_INGEST_KEY.

# Legacy only. The inherited `sweep`, `backfill`, monitor, and enrichment commands still use Firecrawl while
# those routes are replaced. Leave this unset for the native, zero-credit workflow.
# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=

# Optional. A model key turns on the reconcile step: every record is read once more against up to four of its
# sources together, and each field takes the value the sources state, cited to the page and the sentence.
# The first key set wins, in this order; the Vercel AI Gateway is the default (one key, any model:
# https://vercel.com/ai-gateway). Without a key the pipeline runs on Firecrawl alone.
# AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# RECONCILE_MODEL=openai/gpt-6-luna   # the default; any model your provider serves, in its own id form
# One step's own model; unset, the step uses RECONCILE_MODEL.
# RECONCILE_MODEL_FEED=
# RECONCILE_MODEL_CORROBORATE=
# RECONCILE_MODEL_HISTORY=
# RECONCILE_MODEL_ANNOUNCEMENTS=
# RECONCILE_MODEL_REPAIR=
# DOSSIER_VERIFY_MODEL=openai/gpt-6-sol   # checks every dossier claim before it is saved; defaults to RECONCILE_MODEL when set, else this
# RECONCILE_PROVIDER=gateway   # gateway, openai, openrouter, or anthropic: pick one when several keys are set
# RECONCILE=off                          # keep the key, skip the step
# The publication audit: one more model read of a round before it publishes, on the same key. Off turns it off;
# the model defaults to a different family from the read-back. The gate itself is a setting (docs/data-quality.md).
# PUBLICATION_AUDIT=off
# PUBLICATION_AUDIT_MODEL=

# Optional. TypeSafe AI's Jev classifier (https://typesafe.ai) reads each search result's title and snippet
# before a page is paid for: when researching a company's earlier rounds it ranks the company's own
# announcements and journalists' articles first, skips pages that only mention a raise in passing, and never
# lets a funding database or profile verify or date a round. On the live feed it can only add: an article
# the directory-title rule alone declined is read when Jev is sure it announces a round, and every later
# check still applies; it never drops a page the rules keep (`pnpm jev-shadow` compares the two).
# Unset, or on any error, the rules decide alone. `pnpm eval-jev` measures it on labelled results.
# TYPESAFE_API_KEY=

# Optional. Any Postgres 15+. Leave unset to use the embedded PGlite database under ./data.
# A *.neon.tech host is reached over Neon's serverless HTTP driver, anything else over pg. Run `pnpm db:migrate`
# once after setting it. A local Postgres has no TLS, so leave sslmode off its URL; hosted ones usually want it.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres      # docker run -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres postgres:18
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/db?sslmode=require          # hosted Postgres
# DATABASE_DRIVER=neon|pg   # override the choice above (a Neon URL behind a custom domain, or a pg pool on Neon)
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=3       # pg pool size per process; hosts can raise it within their database connection budget
# The pg driver opens a pool of 3 connections per process; on serverless put a pooler in front
# (Neon's pooled URL, PgBouncer). Scripts and `pnpm db:migrate` always use a Neon URL's direct host.

# Optional. Where the embedded PGlite database lives when DATABASE_URL is unset; memory:// keeps it in memory.
# PGLITE_DIR=./data/signalround

# Optional. The bin directory of the PostgreSQL client `pnpm backup-db` runs (psql, pg_dump, pg_restore); default the
# ones on PATH. Its major version must match the server's.
# PG_BIN=/opt/homebrew/opt/postgresql@18/bin

# Optional. How many days `pnpm prune-page-snapshots` keeps stored page snapshots; default 90.
# SNAPSHOT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Optional. Run the test suite against a real Postgres instead of memory (schema dropped and rebuilt per test,
# so run with --no-file-parallelism).
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host/db

# Public deployments. Optional on a laptop. Anyone who can reach the URL can run sweeps, create monitors, and
# press Research or Deep dive, all of which spend Firecrawl credits. On a public deployment set DASHBOARD_PASSWORD
# (or keep the URL private behind Vercel Deployment Protection) and CRON_SECRET, and FIRECRAWL_WEBHOOK_SECRET
# before creating a monitor.

# Recommended on any public deployment. HTTP Basic auth on the dashboard and /api/v1, any username with this
# password. Unset means open.
# DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# Optional. Hosts a public demo: the feed, news, runs, companies, investors, the read API, and the
# feeds (at /feeds/public/...) are open to anyone; Presets, Review, Settings, Deep dive, and every
# other write or Firecrawl call stay behind DASHBOARD_PASSWORD. Off by default.
# PUBLIC_DEMO=1

# Optional. Serves the landing page at / and keeps the dashboard at /feed; the page always answers at /home.
# LANDING=1

# Optional. The company the feed is built on gets a featured strip and its own mark; off turns it off.
# HOUSE_DOMAIN=firecrawl.dev

# Required in production for monitor webhooks. Verifies X-Firecrawl-Signature (Firecrawl dashboard, Advanced
# settings); without it the webhook route refuses unsigned payloads in production.
# FIRECRAWL_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Optional. A bearer token the monitor sends with every webhook, on top of the signature.
# WEBHOOK_BEARER_TOKEN=

# Required in production for anything to run on a schedule. Protects the cron routes: you set it (any random
# string of 16+ characters, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 24`), and Vercel Cron and a production `pnpm start` send it
# on every call. In production the routes refuse every call without one, so nothing sweeps. Optional in dev.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Vercel only. `frequent` runs the cron routes at the full cadence (hourly lanes, quarter-hour verification);
# it needs Vercel Pro. Unset, every route runs once a day, which Hobby accepts. Applies at the next deployment.
# FIREROUND_SCHEDULE=

# The in-process schedule for hosts without Vercel Cron. A production `pnpm start` ticks the cron
# routes itself unless SCHEDULE=0; SCHEDULE=1 arms the same hourly tick under `pnpm dev`.
# SCHEDULE=
# SCHEDULE_TICK_ON_START=1   # also tick once as the schedule arms, instead of waiting for the next hour

# Required for MCP on password-protected private deployments. Clients send this bearer to /api/mcp.
# Without it, a public demo keeps read tools only and a password-protected private instance refuses MCP.
# MCP_API_KEY=

# Required for source-neutral signal ingestion at POST /api/v1/signals. Agent Reach or any other
# discovery worker sends this value as a Bearer token. The route stays unavailable when unset.
# SIGNAL_INGEST_KEY=

# Optional, public demos only. The bearer Firecrawl's Alexandria sends to /api/v1 when it serves the
# fireround provider to agents: its calls skip the per-address budgets and read the same public rows.
# ALEXANDRIA_API_KEY=

# Optional. Production alerts: a Slack incoming webhook for a private channel that receives what /api/cron/health finds.
# Unset, the alerts are logged instead.
# ALERT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Optional. The Firecrawl credits the runs may record in 24 hours before the health check alerts.
# Unset, it alerts past twice the busiest day of the week before.
# CREDIT_ALERT_PER_DAY=

# Optional, public demos. Lets a visitor request research on a company website the feed does not have yet; needs a
# model key for the dossier check. Each address may start three a day; the instance-wide daily cap defaults to 50.
# COMPANY_REQUESTS=1
# COMPANY_REQUESTS_DAILY_MAX=50

# Optional. The email address /privacy, /terms, and /support print for contact; the GitHub issues link is always shown.
# CONTACT_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Optional, for self-hosters. Email digests: an email delivery target collects new rounds and receives them as one daily or weekly
# email through Resend (https://resend.com). Both are needed to send; without them the rows wait in the outbox.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# DIGEST_FROM=Fireround <digest@yourdomain.com>

# Optional. Add to Slack: the Slack app's credentials (Settings > Delivery walks you through creating it).
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Required for Slack delivery targets and public subscriptions. Set before upgrading an existing Slack install.
# The key (32+ characters, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 48`) encrypts webhook URLs and signs public manage links.
# Keep it stable: changing it makes stored credentials unreadable. PUBLIC_DEMO=1 also offers public installs
# with SLACK_CLIENT_ID and SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET from an app with public distribution on (docs/slack.md).
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_KEY=
# Slack app signing secret, used to verify app_uninstalled and tokens_revoked at /api/webhooks/slack.
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# SLACK_SUBSCRIPTION_CAP=500

# Optional. Post significant rounds to the instance's X account (docs/deploy.md#posting-to-x). Off unless set:
# dry_run writes what it would post to the function log (and to the Slack delivery target X_REVIEW_SLACK_TARGET
# names, by its label or id), live posts. The four credentials are the X developer portal's OAuth 1.0a keys for the account,
# with Read and write permission.
# X_POSTING=off
# X_CONSUMER_KEY=
# X_CONSUMER_SECRET=
# X_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# X_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=
# X_MIN_USD_MILLIONS=50
# X_TRENDING_TOP=5
# X_MAX_POSTS_PER_DAY=6
# X_REVIEW_SLACK_TARGET=

# Optional. Public origin used in webhook registrations and Slack links when the request host is not it.
# APP_ORIGIN=https://your-deployment.vercel.app

# Optional. Where search engines should keep the pages when another site serves them under its own address
# (a path is allowed). Canonical tags, og:url, the sitemap's entries, and JSON-LD page URLs use it; unset, APP_ORIGIN.
# SITE_URL=https://www.example.com/funding

# Optional. The User-Agent the Form D lane sends to EDGAR, which asks every caller to name itself and a contact
# (https://www.sec.gov/os/accessing-edgar-data). Unset, the lane sends "fireround/0.1 (contact@example.com)".
# EDGAR_USER_AGENT=fireround/0.1 (you@example.com)

# Optional. Company logos: enrichment stores each company's favicon from its own homepage; a company with a
# domain but no stored logo falls back to a favicon lookup by domain from the reader's browser. Set off to skip it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FAVICON_FALLBACK=off

# Optional. PostHog product analytics, off unless the project key is set: page views and a few named clicks, sent
# through this site's own /ingest path. No cookie, no session recording, Do Not Track honoured (docs/deploy.md).
# The host defaults to PostHog US Cloud; set https://eu.i.posthog.com or your own PostHog's URL instead.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=
